#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721947 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/e8/e889899407c1daa89187078eedc63e91e17c9cb6e4393e74a52c1de69af505e4.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x - 2 y + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one quarter of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-4)$ to $B=(3,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $t=2 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 \\sin{t}\\right)=4 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(2 x - 2 y + 1\\right)\\right|_{x=3 \\cos{t},\\,y=4 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=- 8 \\sin{t} + 6 \\cos{t} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=4\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- 8 \\sin{t} + 6 \\cos{t} + 1\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721954 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/49/4938a35678aa2d7d815af2165204560a743aac43dba7a956b80cf6b61725983e.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is three quarters of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-4)$ to $B=(-4,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 3 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $t=3 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 \\sin{t}\\right)=4 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=4 \\cos{t},\\,y=4 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=16 - 16 \\sin^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=4\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{3 \\pi}\\left(16 - 16 \\sin^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721959 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/42/427a5f3f2ceb974bb7196b7f393252366e9a8cfbd1e1956d7d6286aa86339938.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=(2,-2)$ to $B=(5,-4)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t + 2,\\,- 2 t - 2\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t + 2,\\,- 2 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThe segment parametrization gives $\\mathbf r(0)=(2,-2)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(5,-4)$, so it follows the specified direction.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 t - 2\\right)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=3 t + 2,\\,y=- 2 t - 2}\\\\&=9 t^{2} + 12 t + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=-2\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(9 t^{2} + 12 t + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721961 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/e9/e9bf7c97e66344d2dbe933ac239a5f2cf7ce73ec14272d49d7b0d741127b403a.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,4)$ to $B=(0,-4)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{t},\\,4 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(4 \\sin{t}\\right)=4 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=\\cos{t},\\,y=4 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{t} + \\cos^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=4\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(16 \\sin^{2}{t} + \\cos^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721970 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/8c/8c0a1760eb9218ffd6592bedec96f093ccd2e78df7ad1cc03e4b9c2654aa25c6.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one quarter of the circle $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(-2,0)$ to $B=(0,-2)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{t},\\,2 \\sin{t}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{t},\\,2 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\pi$ and $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\sin{t}\\right)=2 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x y\\right)\\right|_{x=2 \\cos{t},\\,y=2 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=4 \\sin{t} \\cos{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=2\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(4 \\sin{t} \\cos{t}\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721978 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/31/31342567571e1b920d8b3de05e0a858aee18065190987414799674c4824b26d1.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(1 - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the circle $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$, directed clockwise from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(0,-1)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\sin{t},\\,\\cos{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels clockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=0$ and $t=\\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)=- \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(1 - y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=\\sin{t},\\,y=\\cos{t}}\\\\&=1 - \\cos^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=-\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(1 - \\cos^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\sin{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721993 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/9f/9f84ee302059908c7ad624c68ff973343c217aa00033557ff1cc59e0ea1701b2.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is three quarters of the circle $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$, directed clockwise from $A=(-3,0)$ to $B=(0,-3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{t},\\,3 \\cos{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 3 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels clockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $t=3 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 \\cos{t}\\right)=- 3 \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\right|_{x=3 \\sin{t},\\,y=3 \\cos{t}}\\\\&=27 \\sin^{2}{t} \\cos{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=-3\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{3 \\pi}\\left(27 \\sin^{2}{t} \\cos{t}\\right)\\left(\\sin{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721995 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/bd/bd636b841c9c4c5c3eb530eb7da76cf592a82ab899098e5ad97f6da7e4091253.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(3 x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$, directed clockwise from $A=(0,-3)$ to $B=(0,3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\sin{t},\\,3 \\cos{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels clockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\pi$ and $t=2 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 \\cos{t}\\right)=- 3 \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(3 x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=\\sin{t},\\,y=3 \\cos{t}}\\\\&=3 \\sin^{2}{t} + 9 \\cos^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=-3\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left(3 \\sin^{2}{t} + 9 \\cos^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\sin{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721997 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/7d/7dfd81078aecf66c8676276568c629be2daceb63966a0fa1a66dfb9ff1fb4213.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is three quarters of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$, directed clockwise from $A=(0,3)$ to $B=(-4,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\sin{t},\\,3 \\cos{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels clockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 \\cos{t}\\right)=- 3 \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=4 \\sin{t},\\,y=3 \\cos{t}}\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{t} + 9 \\cos^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=-3\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(16 \\sin^{2}{t} + 9 \\cos^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\sin{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722017 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/c6/c6651e5c78464bb97e274f5133362e7754902513683535f3ae6b4334808d1153.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x + 2 y - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(-4,0)$ to $B=(4,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{t},\\,2 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\pi$ and $t=2 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\sin{t}\\right)=2 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(- 2 x + 2 y - 1\\right)\\right|_{x=4 \\cos{t},\\,y=2 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=4 \\sin{t} - 8 \\cos{t} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=2\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left(4 \\sin{t} - 8 \\cos{t} - 1\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722023 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/f3/f395c91bd726bb62a450b0403865283d6a8425ab2d58fc73328c1a1c98211964.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x - 2 y - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,3)$ to $B=(0,-3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{t},\\,3 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 \\sin{t}\\right)=3 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x - 2 y - 1\\right)\\right|_{x=\\cos{t},\\,y=3 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=- 6 \\sin{t} + \\cos{t} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=3\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(- 6 \\sin{t} + \\cos{t} - 1\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722026 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/0f/0fd94a72ccc51b169df4fcdbacffa8ec2d41e6305a3fc6023d7840b4aee6c62b.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one quarter of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-3)$ to $B=(1,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{t},\\,3 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $t=2 \\pi$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(3 \\sin{t}\\right)=3 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=\\cos{t},\\,y=3 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=9 \\sin^{2}{t} + \\cos^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=3\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{t} + \\cos^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722028 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/81/81ceb436a8c1dc03537d69d51dcadf2eeeb5ee382f1bcc9696e34aa5d42bb161.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(4 - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is one half of the ellipse $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-2)$ to $B=(0,2)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization following the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{t},\\,2 \\sin{t}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squared coordinate ratios sum to $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$, so this parametrization lies on the curve. It travels counterclockwise. The endpoints occur at $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}$; the interval covers the specified portion once.\n\nThe signed differential factor is\n\n$$\ny\\prime(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 \\sin{t}\\right)=2 \\cos{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates into the given function gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x(t),y(t))&=\\left.\\left(4 - y^{2}\\right)\\right|_{x=4 \\cos{t},\\,y=2 \\sin{t}}\\\\&=4 - 4 \\sin^{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, retaining the signed derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\&\\quad=2\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}}\\left(4 - 4 \\sin^{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\cos{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722034 :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef353618883e0fa610a179cb3103e9a59cf7bff8f6147317cf65f45bb868b6e6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O$. The polar coordinates of $A$ are $(6,150^\\circ)$ and $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$. Use the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to determine whether the angle of $B$ is greater or less than the angle of $A$.\n\nWhat are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ and $B$ lie on the same circle, $B$ also has radius $6$. The marked arc runs clockwise from $A$ toward $B$, so subtract the included angle:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=150^\\circ-30^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,120^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos120^\\circ=6\\left(-\\frac12\\right)=-3,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin120^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B=(-3,3\\sqrt3)$. The signs agree with its position in quadrant II. The angle $30^\\circ$ is the turn between the two radii, not the polar angle of $B$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722049 :question/problem "![](images/1c/1ca3b41e82afa7c9a6dd513ecc900fc41ec08432027f4fa692836299bf5c3540.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,30^\\circ)$ and $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle to the angle of $A$. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair is correct?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so the radius for $B$ is $r=6$. The marked counterclockwise turn increases the polar angle:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=30^\\circ+30^\\circ=60^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(6,60^\\circ)$. Convert each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos60^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac12\\right)=3,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin60^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,3\\sqrt3)$. Both coordinates are positive, as the diagram requires."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722050 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c450bcb722f49ed4300043f18bc5f8b61c44d25b91312974df4ef8884d9dd8d4.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O$. If the polar coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(6,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}$, determine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The two points lie on the same circle, so the radius for $B$ is $6$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise. Subtract the marked angle, using a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\n=\\frac{10\\pi}{12}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\n=\\frac{3\\pi}{12}=\\frac\\pi4.\n$$\n\nThe polar coordinates of $B$ are therefore $\\left(6,\\frac\\pi4\\right)$. Convert using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt2,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $B=(3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2)$. Its equal positive coordinates agree with the point on the first-quadrant diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722053 :question/problem "![](images/14/140df7664edfa54f0af250561876700abdc3fec016b916cdf463925660a3806b.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ to $B=\\left(2,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 - t,\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=- t + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)} + 3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- t + \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)} + 3\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the trigonometric term, the argument derivative is $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\right)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Keep that argument when applying the sine or cosine primitive.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{2} - \\frac{\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{3 t}{2} + \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722064 :question/problem "![](images/61/61c87cca35f6e0319e2b4e056e505df7795e574983d9c993e1d96182c6c57fe6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(\\sqrt{x} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(1,-1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(0,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1 - t,t - 1\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=1$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 2\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}2 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}2 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t^{2} - 2 t - \\frac{2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722066 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b513d1fd9777406080e133fd9bd62ddce5f14380603498717b001723d011a38.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} + \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(-2,2\\right)$ to $B=\\left(-2,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle -2,2 - 2 t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=\\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right) \\right)} + 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right) \\right)} + 4\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 2 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right) \\right)} - 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the trigonometric term, the argument derivative is $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right)\\right)=- \\pi$. Keep that argument when applying the sine or cosine primitive.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 2 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right) \\right)} - 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- 8 t - 2 \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(1 - t\\right) \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-10\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722071 :question/problem "![](images/93/93ba377ef875077d46f17d69570028d5af7d006edce29832037292554603b074.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x + \\sqrt{y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(2,1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(0,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 - 2 t,1 - t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-1$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=4 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(4 t + \\sqrt{1 - t} - 4\\right)\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 t - \\sqrt{1 - t} + 4\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 t - \\sqrt{1 - t} + 4\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 t^{2} + 4 t + \\frac{2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722072 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d416375ad6b2bccb5cfd28d929d06c42d64dca25dbfb3e21af0384b17a0c7a7e.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x + 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(3,1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(1,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 - 2 t,1 - t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-1$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=- 4 t + 2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2} + 6\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t + 2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2} + 6\\right)\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}4 t - 2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2} - 6\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}4 t - 2 \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2} - 6\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} + 4 t^{2} - 8 t\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{14}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{14}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722092 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/df/df948e65c856771e0497693fa9bc6f941ec68ee0e1fc19ce941d7ae2c0658153.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$, traversed counterclockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(-4,0)$ to $(0,-2)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\pi$ and ends at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+2(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} - 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722100 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/ef/ef0becba15edb6e9745e6fd36a919a018223e5b2d1e015dac806dcbafadcae8b.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(0,-2)$ to $(-4,-3)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 4 t,- t - 2\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "Interpolate from the initial point to the terminal point. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $1$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 4 t,- t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(- 4 t)+(- t - 2)^{2}\\\\&=t^{2} + 4,\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(t^{2} + 4)(-4)\\\\&=- 4 t^{2} - 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1} - 4 t^{2} - 16\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722111 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/f7/f779ac496d7e931e0db1aa172e0901d144a236a44934de69c5223b707f3b5ee3.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=1(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722113 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/de/def37b214cf1509774a63a8fe9c2e33b4ac2ee5225ddbccfa47794e08f01c5eb.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$, traversed clockwise through three quarters of the ellipse from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,0)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and ends at $3 \\pi$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 3 \\pi\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+2(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{3 \\pi} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{3 \\pi} 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722121 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/f6/f6a6a19c5ccbd0b00fcc16e4dd1a06814749f69405182162ea4943439faf5b5d.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=16$, traversed counterclockwise through one quarter of the circle from $(0,-4)$ to $(4,0)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $x^2+y^2=16$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and ends at $2 \\pi$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le 2 \\pi\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=64 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(64 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 256 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} - 256 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722123 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e96f4817f69f22f82be668496ce0b17b4196f70af8a1750924c3b5bda0fe006.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(0,0\\right)$ to $B=\\left(-1,-2\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - t,- 2 t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2 t\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722129 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3d2c8cfb30b17a72254cc17f29ce88a1921a1358981c09f726bf06395308789.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(2,3\\right)$ to $B=\\left(0,2\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 - 2 t,3 - t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-1$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=- 2 t - 2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 2 t - 2\\right)\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}2 t + 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}2 t + 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722145 :question/problem "![](images/73/736c4db27d66b2ca39e24b91abdc42df8ae71749c4e99daf509414c7ef402d82.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(-5,3\\right)$ to $B=\\left(-2,0\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t - 5,3 - 3 t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-3$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=9 t - 11\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(9 t - 11\\right)\\left(-3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}33 - 27 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}33 - 27 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{27 t^{2}}{2} + 33 t\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{39}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722146 :question/problem "![](images/54/545abf42aefd8d15703830dab18ace4d744fef351818e8ee4d9101d2052b26e7.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(1,-1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(3,1\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t + 1,2 t - 1\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=6 t - 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(6 t - 1\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[6 t^{2} - 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722162 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/3d/3da9ed3b002a954b70f4aae8a7ee862682c8379688863b87dd49dea16e4eb905.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $16 x^{2} + y^{2}=16$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{1}{2},- 2 \\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-2)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722167 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/91/91c0bb3ccbb239ce4a24ad76245345da38cd29e495475bd8d306132af414b1eb.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed counterclockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722169 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/31/310cd83111af3ba8b0cb57f3ab0268b69eff07244a22f36116ecde25faf73877.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$ to $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\sqrt{3})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and ends at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le t\\le \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722170 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/b5/b5ab08b1bd65b8c691bcbb7e59820f20ff9b6e226d8590efe10c6656e8dab9d5.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=4$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(1,- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ to $(- \\sqrt{3},- \\frac{1}{2})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722190 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/5e/5ee33fbf6c4e2e19b50e3ba4044892313aa1b1ede922cfa1d6fea668d4169181.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=9$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the circle from $(-3,0)$ to $(0,3)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $x^2+y^2=9$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\pi$ and ends at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 27 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} - 27 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722196 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/f2/f24264686520059e61af6a8d15976af7ac329ccf98ad3f35639e96ac0583d5b7.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(1,-2)$ to $(5,-4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Interpolate from the initial point to the terminal point. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $1$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 1,- 2 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(- 2 t - 2)e^{(4 t + 1)}\\\\&=- 2 t e^{4 t + 1} - 2 e^{4 t + 1},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 2 t e^{4 t + 1} - 2 e^{4 t + 1})(4)\\\\&=- 8 t e^{4 t + 1} - 8 e^{4 t + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1} - 8 t e^{4 t + 1} - 8 e^{4 t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722199 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/9e/9ec46a661c229864a208a35018ef87451a0bb08ccc9500773dd66cf3c5ab36ab.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=16$, traversed counterclockwise through one half of the circle from $(0,-4)$ to $(0,4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $x^2+y^2=16$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})-(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722200 :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/5c/5c2e62972898afdd07d268db0e21a6c0ff3677ddb8b33339b53204498c540cfd.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(6,-4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Interpolate from the initial point to the terminal point. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $1$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- 3 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 t + 2)+(- 3 t - 1)^{2}\\\\&=9 t^{2} + 10 t + 3,\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(9 t^{2} + 10 t + 3)(4)\\\\&=36 t^{2} + 40 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1} 36 t^{2} + 40 t + 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722223 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\sin(2t)-1,\\qquad y=\\cos(3t)+2,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe indicated segment is shown below.\n\n![Parametric curve segment with direction and parameter endpoints](images/39/397d64be37483a28368c36a7bef340c511c9aff9a7e6160e727ba71ea22a18d5.png)\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2\\cos(2t),\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-3\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nSubstitute the derivatives and the parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\sqrt{(2\\cos(2t))^2+(-3\\sin(3t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\sqrt{4\\cos^2(2t)+9\\sin^2(3t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constants $-1$ and $2$ have zero derivatives. The chain rule supplies factors $2$ and $3$, which become $4$ and $9$ when squared. Squaring the negative derivative gives a positive term. The arguments $2t$ and $3t$ differ, so the two squared trigonometric terms cannot be combined using the Pythagorean identity. Keep the parameter limits $0$ and $\\pi/3$ and leave the integral unevaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722226 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=e^t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThe indicated segment is shown below.\n\n![Parametric curve segment with direction and parameter endpoints](images/9d/9d6b750b612ea841f5afb9bc37ed3d774d3051c77a13e2cb691218c051b1d3b6.png)\n\nUse the arc-length formula\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=e^t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the derivatives and the parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_0^1\\sqrt{1^2+(e^t)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^1\\sqrt{1+e^{2t}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative and add the squares inside the square root. In particular, $(e^t)^2=e^{2t}$. The integration limits are the given parameter values, $0$ and $1$. This integral is the requested expression; no evaluation is needed."}]}
